<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Gold''s July 3 local high near $4,163 preceded both the July 7 tanker attack and the July 12 to 13 strikes. The yield series reflects the daily close for the benchmark US 10 year Treasury note.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients who hold gold as geopolitical insurance are confused, not panicked. They read the weekend headlines, expected the position to work the way it worked in March and April, and are now looking at a two week low instead. The dominant emotion is closer to doubt about the strategy than fear about the war itself.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Gold fell Monday, and I want to walk through why, because it is not the reaction most people expect after a weekend like this one. The metal is not down because the Hormuz situation improved. It is down because the market is spending this week pricing Kevin Warsh''s first Congressional testimony and Wednesday''s inflation data more heavily than it is pricing the strait. Gold reacts most predictably to a single, dominant threat. Right now it is caught between two, and the rate story is winning. That does not make gold a bad hedge. It means gold is currently trading on a different variable than the one you added it for, and that can persist for a few weeks before it resolves. I am not recommending a change to the position today. I want you to understand what you are actually holding through.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients who added or increased gold allocations specifically as a Hormuz or Middle East hedge in March through May.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with a long standing strategic gold allocation of 5 to 10% who are watching short term price action without a specific event driving their original decision.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with energy sector exposure through Suncor, Canadian Natural Resources, or Cenovus, who are seeing the more conventional side of this weekend''s move play out in the way it usually does.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients who added gold specifically citing the Hormuz conflict as the rationale, using notes from March through May review meetings</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review whether any client is close to a rebalancing trigger that would force a gold sale at the current local low</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients scheduled for a call this week ahead of Wednesday''s Warsh testimony and BoC decision</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversations, noting that no allocation change was recommended based on a single week of price action</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why gold moved the opposite way this weekend<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, here is the short version in writing. Gold fell Monday despite the fresh escalation between the US and Iran over the Strait of Hormuz, because this week''s dominant story for investors is Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh''s first Congressional testimony and Wednesday''s inflation data, not the Gulf. A stronger case for a rate hike weighs on gold more than a war headline lifts it right now.<br><br>
This is not a signal to change your allocation, and I am not recommending one today. It is a reminder that gold can trade on more than one variable at a time, and the variable in charge can shift within the same month. I will follow up once we have clarity from Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision and the Warsh testimony.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY gold holders:</strong> Self directed investors who bought gold ETFs or bullion as a war hedge and have no framework for interpreting a counterintuitive move like Monday''s decline.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated commodity investors:</strong> Prospects overweight gold or energy without a coordinated view of how the two are diverging right now.</p>
<p><strong>Clients anxious ahead of Wednesday:</strong> Investors aware that a Fed testimony and a Bank of Canada decision are landing in the same 48 hour window and unsure how to position for either.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Did you notice gold fell Monday even though Iran just closed the Strait of Hormuz again. Most people expected the opposite, and the reason it did not happen tells you a lot about what is actually driving markets this week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor watching gold fall during a war escalation has no one to explain the mechanism, and the instinct is often to assume the hedge failed and abandon it at the worst possible moment. The advisor''s value here is narrow and specific: explaining that gold is currently pricing Federal Reserve rate expectations more heavily than geopolitical risk, and that this can reverse the moment Wednesday''s testimony and CPI data land. That is not a generic reassurance. It is a read on which of two competing threats the market is weighing this week, delivered before the client makes an irreversible decision based on one bad week.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>What was gold supposed to protect your portfolio against when you first bought it?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through why gold and oil have been moving in opposite directions the past two weeks?</p>
<p>Are you positioned for Wednesday, when the Fed chair testifies and the Bank of Canada announces its rate decision on the same day?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Gold fell during a war escalation. Here is why.<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Gold dropped 1.39% Monday, the same day the US and Iran traded another wave of strikes over the Strait of Hormuz and Tehran declared it closed again. If you hold gold as portfolio insurance, that move probably did not make sense.<br><br>
It comes down to which threat the market is pricing this week: a Federal Reserve chair testifying before Congress for the first time, with inflation data landing the same days, versus a war the market has already priced several times over. I would be glad to walk through what that means for your specific position before Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
