VALUES (
  '2026/07/13/fed-hawkish-tilt-boc-hold-warsh-testimony',
  'economy', 'article',
  'The Fed''s Internal Vote Just Turned Hawkish. The Bank of Canada Has Not Moved. Wednesday Puts Both on Display.', 'June''s FOMC minutes showed nine of twelve officials favouring higher US rates, the first hawkish signal of Kevin Warsh''s chairmanship. The Bank of Canada meets the same day, expected to hold for a sixth consecutive time. The distance between the two paths is the story this week.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh testifies before Congress Tuesday and Wednesday,</strong><span> delivering his first semiannual Monetary Policy Report as chair the same week the Bank of Canada announces its July rate decision.</span></li>
<li><strong>Minutes from the June 16 to 17 FOMC meeting, the first chaired by Warsh,</strong><span> showed nine of twelve officials favouring higher rates before year end, with six of those forecasting two separate quarter point hikes.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada is widely expected to hold its overnight rate at 2.25%</strong><span> on July 15 for a sixth consecutive meeting, unchanged since the Bank''s October 2025 cut.</span></li>
<li><strong>The US 10 year Treasury yield has climbed toward 4.59%,</strong><span> near a two month high, while markets price roughly a 60% probability of a September Fed rate increase.</span></li>
<li><strong>The mechanism that matters for Canadian households</strong><span> runs from this week''s decisions through the five year Government of Canada bond yield to fixed mortgage rates, ahead of the 2026 to 2027 renewal wall.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Federal Reserve Chair Kevin Warsh testifies before the House Financial Services Committee Tuesday and the Senate Banking Committee Wednesday, delivering his first semiannual Monetary Policy Report to Congress since taking the chair in June. The Bank of Canada announces its own rate decision Wednesday morning, hours before Warsh appears before the Senate. Neither event is expected to change a headline rate this week. Together, they confirm that the two central banks are no longer moving in the same direction.</p>
<h2>What the Fed''s Internal Vote Actually Showed</h2>
<p>Minutes from the June 16 to 17 FOMC meeting, the first chaired by Warsh, showed the committee holding the federal funds rate at 3.50% to 3.75%, unanimously, while a clear internal majority argued for tightening. Nine of twelve officials favoured higher rates before year end, and six of those pencilled in two separate quarter point hikes rather than one. The minutes also marked the first time the committee incorporated artificial intelligence infrastructure investment into its inflation discussion, flagging it as a demand side pressure alongside the more familiar energy channel from the Middle East conflict.</p>
<p>The Fed''s preferred inflation gauge, core PCE, ran at 3.4% year over year as of the most recent reading, nearly double the 2% target. Markets are pricing roughly a 60% probability of a rate increase at the September meeting. Warsh''s first Monetary Policy Report, released ahead of this week''s testimony, gave unusual prominence to the M2 money supply, a monetarist framework that has been absent from Fed communication for decades and signals a deliberate shift in how this Fed chair intends to explain policy.</p>
<h2>The Bank of Canada Has Not Moved Since October</h2>
<p>The Bank of Canada''s overnight rate has sat at 2.25% since an October 29, 2025 cut, through holds on January 28, March 18, April 29, and June 10. Wednesday''s decision is widely expected to be a sixth consecutive hold. Governor Tiff Macklem has described the setting as a dilemma: soft domestic growth, with first quarter GDP contracting 0.1%, sits against elevated near term inflation running near 3%, driven by oil prices that remain roughly ten dollars above the Bank''s April assumptions.</p>
<p>That dilemma keeps the Bank of Canada genuinely two sided, debating whether the next move is a cut or a hike, not leaning toward tightening the way nine of twelve FOMC voters just did. The weekend''s fresh escalation in the Strait of Hormuz, with Brent crude back above 79 dollars, reintroduces exactly the inflation risk that had been fading from the Bank''s calculus heading into this week, without yet being enough to change Wednesday''s expected outcome.</p>
<h2>Where This Reaches a Five Year Mortgage</h2>
<p>The transmission that matters for Canadian households does not require the Bank of Canada to move at all. Fixed mortgage rates track the bond market, not the overnight rate directly, and the five year Government of Canada yield responds to global capital flows as much as to domestic policy. The US 10 year Treasury yield has climbed toward 4.59%, near a two month high, while Canada''s 10 year sits at 3.55%, its highest level since May. As the spread between them widens on the back of the Fed''s hawkish internal tilt, Canadian yields get pulled higher even if the Bank of Canada holds every meeting through the rest of the year.</p>
<p>That matters most for the wave of Canadian mortgages renewing over 2026 and into 2027, originated when five year fixed rates sat meaningfully lower. A borrower renewing this fall faces a rate environment shaped less by what the Bank of Canada announces Wednesday than by what nine Federal Reserve officials just told markets they want to do next.</p>
<p>The distribution below reflects each FOMC participant''s individual rate path preference from the June meeting, set against the Bank of Canada''s unchanged policy setting over the same period.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
<div style="background:#ffffff;border:1px solid #d0d0d0;width:100%;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
<div style="background:#f5f5f5;border-bottom:1px solid #d0d0d0;padding:10px 14px;display:flex;align-items:baseline;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;">
<span style="font-size:13px;font-weight:700;color:#111;letter-spacing:0.02em;">FOMC JUNE 2026 | INDIVIDUAL RATE PATH PREFERENCE</span>
<span style="font-size:20px;font-weight:700;color:#111;">9 of 12</span>
<span style="font-size:13px;color:#c0392b;">&#9650; FAVOUR HIKES</span>
<span style="font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-left:auto;">JUNE 16 TO 17 MEETING &nbsp;|&nbsp; FIRST WARSH CHAIRED FOMC</span>
</div>
<div style="padding:12px 14px 8px;">
<script>
(function(){
var _cs = document.currentScript;
function el(tag, attrs, txt){
  var e = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", tag);
  for (var k in attrs){ e.setAttribute(k, attrs[k]); }
  if (txt !== undefined && txt !== null){ e.textContent = txt; }
  return e;
}
// 12 FOMC officials, June 16-17 2026 meeting: preferred additional 2026 hikes
// 3 favour hold/cut (0), 3 favour one hike, 6 favour two hikes
var dotPrefs = [0,0,0,1,1,1,2,2,2,2,2,2];
var n = dotPrefs.length;
var labels = ["HOLD OR CUT","ONE HIKE","TWO HIKES"];

var margin = {left:62, right:24, top:18, bottom:46};
var PW = 594, PH = 236;
var colW = PW/3;

function xCol(v){ return margin.left + colW*v + colW/2; }

var svg = document.createElementNS("http://www.w3.org/2000/svg","svg");
svg.setAttribute("viewBox","0 0 680 300");

// Column separators
for (var c=1;c<3;c++){
  var sx = margin.left + colW*c;
  svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:sx,x2:sx,y1:margin.top,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#ececec","stroke-width":"0.5"}));
}
// baseline
svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:margin.top+PH,y2:margin.top+PH,stroke:"#d8d8d8","stroke-width":"1"}));

// column labels
for (var cl=0; cl<3; cl++){
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xCol(cl),y:margin.top+PH+16,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#999999"}, labels[cl]));
}

// stack dots per column
var counts = {0:0,1:0,2:0};
var dotR = 9;
var dotGap = 4;
for (var i=0;i<n;i++){
  var col = dotPrefs[i];
  var idx = counts[col];
  counts[col] = idx+1;
  var cx = xCol(col);
  var cy = margin.top+PH - dotR - idx*(dotR*2+dotGap);
  var fillColor = col === 2 ? "#8a3030" : (col === 1 ? "#4a5568" : "#9ca3af");
  svg.appendChild(el("circle",{cx:cx,cy:cy,r:dotR,fill:fillColor,"fill-opacity":"0.88"}));
}

// count labels above each stack
[0,1,2].forEach(function(col){
  var topY = margin.top+PH - dotR - (counts[col]-1)*(dotR*2+dotGap) - dotR - 8;
  svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:xCol(col),y:topY,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"11","font-weight":"700","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#111111"}, counts[col]+" of 12"));
});

// Reference annotation: BoC stance, placed as a text box in upper left of plot
svg.appendChild(el("line",{x1:margin.left,x2:margin.left+PW,y1:margin.top+14,y2:margin.top+14,stroke:"#1a3560","stroke-opacity":"0.4","stroke-dasharray":"3,3"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:margin.left+6,y:margin.top+11,"text-anchor":"start","font-size":"7","font-weight":"700","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#1a3560"}, "BOC: SIXTH CONSECUTIVE HOLD EXPECTED, NO TILT EITHER WAY"));

// Gold pill on the dominant reading (two hikes column, most significant)
var pillW = 100, pillH = 16;
var domX = xCol(2);
var domTopY = margin.top+PH - dotR - (counts[2]-1)*(dotR*2+dotGap) - dotR - 26;
var pillX = domX - pillW/2;
var pillY = domTopY - pillH - 4;
if (pillY < margin.top+18) pillY = margin.top+18;
svg.appendChild(el("rect",{x:pillX,y:pillY,width:pillW,height:pillH,rx:3,fill:"#e8a825"}));
svg.appendChild(el("text",{x:pillX+pillW/2,y:pillY+pillH/2+4,"text-anchor":"middle","font-size":"8","font-weight":"700","font-family":"-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif",fill:"#111111"}, "MOST COMMON VIEW"));

var container = _cs ? _cs.parentNode : null;
if (container) container.appendChild(svg);
})();
</script>
</div>
<div style="font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:10px;color:#999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: Federal Reserve FOMC June 2026 meeting minutes; Bank of Canada policy rate schedule, July 2026. &nbsp;|&nbsp; hdq.ca</div>
</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Each dot represents one FOMC participant''s individually stated preference for additional 2026 rate moves, as reflected in the June meeting minutes. The Bank of Canada reference reflects its policy rate setting over the same period, unchanged since October 2025.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with upcoming mortgage renewals are anxious about a Bank of Canada decision that, on its own, is unlikely to move their rate at all. The anxiety is directionally correct but attached to the wrong event. The more relevant signal this week comes from Washington, not Ottawa.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The Bank of Canada is expected to hold rates again Wednesday, and that part is not the story. The real development this week is what came out of the Fed''s June meeting minutes: nine of twelve officials now want higher US rates, and six of them are pencilling in two hikes before year end. That is the first clearly hawkish signal since Kevin Warsh took over as Fed chair, and it is already pulling US and Canadian bond yields higher together. If you are renewing your mortgage this fall, the rate you are quoted will be shaped more by that shift than by anything the Bank of Canada announces this week.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with fixed rate mortgages renewing in the next six to eighteen months, who assumed the Bank of Canada''s hold meant a stable rate environment.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with variable rate mortgages or HELOCs, whose rates track the Bank of Canada''s overnight rate directly and are less exposed to this specific US driven yield move.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Fixed income clients holding shorter duration Government of Canada bonds, who benefit from the yield increase on new purchases or reinvestment.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with mortgage renewals scheduled in the next twelve months and flag files for a rate environment conversation</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review fixed income allocations for clients who may benefit from locking in current five year Government of Canada yields</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Prepare a short note distinguishing Wednesday''s BoC decision from the more consequential Warsh testimony for client communications</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversations noting no allocation changes were made based on this week''s testimony alone</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Why this week''s Fed testimony matters more than the BoC decision<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, here is the short version. The Bank of Canada is expected to hold rates Wednesday for a sixth consecutive meeting, which on its own should not move your rate. The more relevant development is that nine of twelve Federal Reserve officials now favour higher US rates, the first hawkish signal since Kevin Warsh became Fed chair, and it is already pulling bond yields higher on both sides of the border.<br><br>
For anyone renewing a fixed rate mortgage in the coming months, this US driven yield move matters more than Wednesday''s Canadian announcement. I will follow up once we have Warsh''s testimony and the BoC''s Monetary Policy Report in hand.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY mortgage shoppers:</strong> Self directed borrowers watching the Bank of Canada announcement for a signal, unaware that this week''s more important event is a Congressional testimony in Washington.</p>
<p><strong>Fixed income DIY investors:</strong> Prospects managing their own bond ladders without a framework for interpreting the widening US Canada yield spread.</p>
<p><strong>Renewal window clients:</strong> Homeowners with mortgages maturing in the next year who have not yet engaged an advisor on rate strategy.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Everyone is watching Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision, but the more important event this week is happening in Washington, where nine of twelve Fed officials just told markets they want higher rates. If you are renewing a mortgage soon, that is the signal that actually matters.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY borrower or investor tracking only the Bank of Canada''s headline decision will miss the more consequential shift happening in the FOMC''s internal vote and its effect on bond yields that feed directly into fixed mortgage pricing. The advisor''s value is distinguishing the event that will move headlines Wednesday from the event that is already moving rates, and translating that into a concrete recommendation on renewal timing or fixed income positioning before the client acts on the wrong signal.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Are you watching Wednesday''s Bank of Canada announcement for guidance on your mortgage renewal?</p>
<p>Has anyone explained why US Treasury yields matter more than the Bank of Canada''s rate for your fixed mortgage pricing?</p>
<p>Do you know when your current mortgage or bond holdings are next up for renewal or reinvestment?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The rate signal that actually matters this week is not the BoC<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Nine of twelve Federal Reserve officials just told markets they want higher US rates, the first hawkish signal since Kevin Warsh became Fed chair. Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision is expected to be a non event by comparison, but bond yields on both sides of the border are already moving on the Fed''s shift.<br><br>
If you have a mortgage renewal coming up or manage your own fixed income, this is worth a conversation before the next rate quote lands. I would be glad to walk through what it means for your specific situation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
