</div>
<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">Prices reflect intraday and settlement readings reported across multiple sessions. The June 22 to early July decline coincided with the June 17 ceasefire memorandum between the US and Iran.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with energy exposure feel validated by Monday''s move and want to know if this is finally the sustained breakout they have been waiting for. Clients without energy exposure feel like they are watching the same headline cycle repeat for the fourth time and are inclined to dismiss it entirely. Both reactions are understandable and both are incomplete.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Oil jumped again this weekend after the US and Iran traded another round of strikes, and Brent has now round tripped back to where it was three weeks ago before the ceasefire took hold. I want to be precise about what that means. The base case, based on the last three escalations, is that this fades within one to two weeks. The reason I am not dismissing it outright is that the physical shipping data, not the headlines, shows a real slowdown in traffic through the strait, and each escalation this month has pushed the price to a slightly higher peak than the one before it. That is not yet a trend I would reposition around, but it is one I am watching closely.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated exposure to Canadian integrated energy producers, who benefit from near term price strength but face the same reversal risk if the pattern holds and this fades like the prior three episodes.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with broad diversified portfolios where energy is a sector weighting rather than a concentrated position.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients holding Canadian energy positions specifically as a geopolitical hedge, who are seeing that thesis validated this week even if gold, the more conventional hedge, is not.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review energy sector weightings for clients ahead of Wednesday''s BoC decision and Warsh testimony, both of which will interact with this week''s oil move</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with concentrated Canadian energy positions for a conversation distinguishing base case fade risk from tail risk persistence</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the shipping traffic data point, nine vessels against a typical 30 to 40, as a talking point distinct from the closed or open rhetoric</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document today''s conversations as monitoring, not repositioning, absent confirmation the pattern has genuinely changed</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> What this weekend''s escalation means, and what it does not yet mean<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Following our call, here is the short version. Brent crude rose more than 4% Monday after a fourth wave of US strikes on Iran and Iranian retaliation against Bahrain, Kuwait, and Jordan. Oil has now round tripped back to its June 22 level in under three weeks.<br><br>
The base case, consistent with the past three escalations since the ceasefire, is that this fades within one to two weeks. The reason we are watching rather than dismissing it is that actual shipping traffic through the strait has genuinely slowed, and each escalation this month has pushed prices to a higher peak than the last. No changes to your positioning today. I will follow up as the data develops.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY energy traders:</strong> Self directed investors trading Canadian energy names on headlines alone, without a framework for distinguishing this week''s move from the prior three fade cycles.</p>
<p><strong>Concentrated single sector holders:</strong> Prospects with outsized energy exposure and no plan for what happens if this pattern finally breaks in either direction.</p>
<p><strong>Clients confused by the gold and oil divergence:</strong> Investors who expected both assets to move together on a war headline and are seeing them diverge instead.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">Brent crude just round tripped back to its pre ceasefire level in under three weeks. If you hold energy names or are thinking about it, the question that actually matters is not whether oil is up today, it is whether this is the fourth fade or the first real break in the pattern.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A DIY investor reacting to Monday''s headline has no framework for separating this move from the three prior escalations that all faded within weeks. The advisor''s value is precision: distinguishing the base case, informed by the actual pattern since the ceasefire, from the tail risk, informed by physical shipping data the headlines are not reporting, and translating that into a specific recommendation on sector weighting rather than a reaction to the news cycle.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you currently positioned in Canadian energy, and was that a deliberate decision or something that happened to your portfolio over time?</p>
<p>Do you have a plan for what you would do if this week''s oil spike fades within two weeks, the way the past three have?</p>
<p>Has anyone walked you through the physical shipping data behind the Hormuz headlines, rather than just the price reaction?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Oil just round tripped in three weeks. Here is the part the headlines miss.<br><br>
Hi [Name],<br><br>
Brent crude jumped more than 4% Monday after a fourth wave of US strikes on Iran, and the benchmark has now round tripped back to its June 22 level in under three weeks. The base case, based on the pattern since the ceasefire, is that this fades again. The physical shipping data behind it is more interesting than the headline.<br><br>
If you hold Canadian energy positions or are considering it, I would be glad to walk through what the actual data supports, separate from the rhetoric on either side.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
