VALUES (
  '2026/07/13/tsx-record-distance-catalyst-week-oil-surge',
  'market', 'article',
  '324 Points From a Record, the TSX Heads Into Its Busiest 48 Hours of the Summer', 'Friday''s close left the index within 0.9% of its June 17 high. Tuesday''s US inflation data, Wednesday''s Bank of Canada decision, and Warsh''s first Congressional testimony now land inside a single 48 hour window, against a backdrop of oil already up more than 4% Monday.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>The TSX closed at 35,305.31 Friday,</strong><span> up 0.298% on the day and within 0.9% of its June 17 record of 35,629.89, after Canada''s June jobs report beat expectations with 18,200 net new positions.</span></li>
<li><strong>Brent crude has risen more than 4% in Monday trading</strong><span> to above 79 dollars a barrel following a fourth wave of US strikes on Iran, setting up a strong open for TSX energy names.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Cboe Volatility Index closed Friday at 15.57,</strong><span> a level that reflects little options market pricing for the density of catalysts landing this week.</span></li>
<li><strong>Gold fell 1.39% Monday to a two week low</strong><span> even as the geopolitical backdrop deteriorated, while the US 10 year Treasury yield climbed toward a two month high near 4.59%.</span></li>
<li><strong>Three catalysts land inside a single 48 hour window this week:</strong><span> June CPI and Warsh''s House testimony Tuesday, and Warsh''s Senate testimony alongside the Bank of Canada''s rate decision Wednesday.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The TSX closed at 35,305.31 Friday, up 0.298%, within 0.9% of its June 17 record of 35,629.89. The index closed the week on strength from Canada''s June jobs report, which added 18,200 positions against a 10,000 forecast and pushed unemployment down to 6.5%, tied for the lowest reading in nearly two years.</p>
<h2>Oil Sets Up Monday''s Energy Trade</h2>
<p>Brent crude has jumped more than 4% in Monday trading to above 79 dollars a barrel, its highest level since June 22, after the US launched a fourth wave of strikes against Iran over the weekend. WTI has moved in step, up close to 3% toward 74 dollars. The last time a Hormuz escalation of comparable direction moved Brent by roughly 2%, on July 7, Imperial Oil, Strathcona Resources, and Suncor each gained more than 2% within the first hour of trading. Monday''s move in Brent is more than double the size of that one.</p>
<h2>A Market Pricing Calm Into a Loud Week</h2>
<p>The Cboe Volatility Index closed Friday at 15.57, a level that reflects little options market pricing for the density of catalysts landing this week. June CPI data and Kevin Warsh''s first House testimony land Tuesday. Warsh''s Senate testimony and the Bank of Canada''s rate decision land Wednesday, within hours of each other. A VIX in the low teens into that calendar is either confidence the outcomes are already priced, or complacency the bond market does not share.</p>
<p>The bond market is not showing the same calm. The US 10 year Treasury yield has climbed toward 4.59%, near a two month high, while Canada''s 10 year sits at 3.55%, its highest level since May. Equities are pricing a quiet week. Rates are not.</p>
<h2>What Moved and What Did Not</h2>
<p>Gold fell 1.39% Monday to 4,063.91 dollars, a two week low, even as the geopolitical backdrop that would normally support it deteriorated further. The Canadian dollar closed Friday at 1.4132 per US dollar, having rebounded from a 15 month low of 1.425 on June 28 after the strong jobs print. That rebound faces renewed pressure Monday from both the oil driven inflation angle and a widening US Canada yield spread working against the loonie.</p>
<p>The index has traded in a narrowing band beneath its June 17 peak for three weeks, and Monday''s oil driven energy strength arrives as the most likely catalyst to test whether that ceiling holds.</p>

<div class="hdq-chart">
